#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4435e346-d6ad-58cd-97cb-bbd45e121759" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "12811582-7156-5b84-9ba9-5545f87894f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3993" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "At first, it may not look like we have two factors. Actually, we do, but one of them is implicit. The trick is to write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\ln{x} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing this trick, we can now use integration by parts. We let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the by-parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+C \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096475 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31a28451-005e-50dc-a7cf-5b666b1d7735" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "c7373a16-c90a-5875-8b17-8724542b35ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-493" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{e} 4x\\ln\\left(x^3\\right) \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and $v',$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot (2{x}^{2}){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}(2{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{(e)}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})-2{(1)}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{3})]-6{\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}-6(\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}-3{x}^{2}{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}-3({e}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "672d52f6-89d8-550d-863b-7c5157d59ca3" :question/math-academy-id "e-494" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\log_4{x}}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{4}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡x(-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,{log}_{4}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula $\\log_4{x}=\\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{\\ln{4}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58138fa6-f88b-5fe5-af1a-d57a9ddf92d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257544} {:answer/id #uuid "598adb7f-4f91-5bfa-90ed-fbfcd560f2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257557} {:answer/id #uuid "656c4472-5467-578e-a27b-48ffcf468dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257676} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e9394b-cbea-5ee1-b925-bbc5ca528c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258192} {:answer/id #uuid "bef4b94b-4728-5d37-9b37-3a8a605b16fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bef4b94b-4728-5d37-9b37-3a8a605b16fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258446} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbae28f0-aedd-5317-b5f3-02a112b05036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d30efc2-05d5-57a7-a888-f5dbfc3f16de" :question/math-academy-id "q-12566" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡(x))(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47d0d172-5a30-57d5-a766-d99669bdec9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257411} {:answer/id #uuid "5650ea15-d618-5b47-8c28-c84f1b05c79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257525} {:answer/id #uuid "b25a9222-222c-5e15-ba09-a0c57522e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258336} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b91501-5eb2-51d9-8768-15ceeaa9babf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258785} {:answer/id #uuid "eec4b01f-b497-53f1-8b34-4b8b2d67f6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec4b01f-b497-53f1-8b34-4b8b2d67f6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258851} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b1e6c3d-3d86-5dab-9cbb-0d0320668747" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8f0b656-4bc9-55ad-bafd-83e444f61957" :question/math-academy-id "q-12568" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(3x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31a28451-005e-50dc-a7cf-5b666b1d7735" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2dedfa-ce0e-5837-9aa6-948ae0fbefbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{49}(13{e}^{14}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a2dedfa-ce0e-5837-9aa6-948ae0fbefbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{49}(13{e}^{14}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319717} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91ebf90-9329-5211-901e-f7e1002ebff9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e85cfc96-f5dd-5d18-a1c7-6c52affb5618" :question/math-academy-id "q-350965" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}4{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=4{x}^{6} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 4{x}^{6}\\,dx=\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}4{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{7}){|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{7})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{4({e}^{2})^{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{7}-\\frac{4(1)^{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{7}]-\\frac{4}{7}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{14}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{7}-\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{49}{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{14}}{7}-(\\frac{4{e}^{14}}{49}-\\frac{4}{49}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{49}(56{e}^{14}-4{e}^{14}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{49}(13{e}^{14}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4435e346-d6ad-58cd-97cb-bbd45e121759" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f00d9a8d-19f9-59ab-a52c-04f26061eaeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f00d9a8d-19f9-59ab-a52c-04f26061eaeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320111} :answer-field/id #uuid "f261cb5e-8b25-581f-a45f-89fb8e53daa1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4539aae-02db-5cfd-beb0-0a96bbadcc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-350967" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/2}^{e/2}-{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,(x){|}_{1/2}^{e/2}-{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}x(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{e}{2})\\,(\\frac{e}{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\,(\\frac{1}{2})]-{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-x{|}_{1/2}^{e/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}e-\\frac{1}{2}e+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d858571-c742-53a0-8b0c-77739cb65295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346306} {:answer/id #uuid "8feedb2c-04a2-53e5-977e-8f184d2cf677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346603} {:answer/id #uuid "cbba3343-135c-5346-b20a-1d17da2aa7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347136} {:answer/id #uuid "e04a9d15-7ecd-52b0-bb90-8771171d09a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347319} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f50154-55d7-5b5d-b090-604eeaada9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbba3343-135c-5346-b20a-1d17da2aa7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347136} :answer-field/id #uuid "3798b5c2-73c3-5866-99de-0b57f29d4d28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ded2d95-c1cd-581b-8bd9-c8df53884512" :question/math-academy-id "q-78963" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 9{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ {v}^{′}=9{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\cdot (3{x}^{3})-\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {x}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f9a77b-5187-5f1e-91ab-58857644655e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345794} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff153a9-ddfd-5137-be96-19b18d9528c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346472} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bb072c-6dc8-522d-8e1e-52a3e26e7e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346932} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6a35da-5e50-5868-9ffe-c00ba27afa4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347130} {:answer/id #uuid "f121c972-8299-519a-85af-8e6e76287a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5bb072c-6dc8-522d-8e1e-52a3e26e7e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346932} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e201519-7ef2-5188-bf15-bd65e9d10b4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9218e349-d846-5b44-8076-17797f61e65a" :question/math-academy-id "q-78964" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 25{x}^{4}{log}_{5}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ {v}^{′}=25{x}^{4}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 25{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=5{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}{log}_{5}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(5x)\\cdot (5{x}^{5})-\\int \\frac{5{x}^{5}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{log}_{5}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int 5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{log}_{5}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot {x}^{5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{5}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4435e346-d6ad-58cd-97cb-bbd45e121759" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fac7b1b-401e-571f-9ac1-c8035cba4079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424984} {:answer/id #uuid "612aede7-2fcb-5f05-9a9e-41d69e0a1e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424985} {:answer/id #uuid "92b952c7-daa6-5ab4-a7f5-81b3cf909d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424986} {:answer/id #uuid "bd63e3cd-ff82-5ade-9211-594e2bfdb08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424987} {:answer/id #uuid "cc74e90b-cf7b-5156-89e6-293ad43d67ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "612aede7-2fcb-5f05-9a9e-41d69e0a1e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424985} :answer-field/id #uuid "8afc707b-a42b-5621-ace9-e6a014a201bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45517c4f-34c5-5613-91b3-714b64a55e54" :question/math-academy-id "q-78972" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡2x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-x+C \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant."}]}
